.TH LLAPI_FIND_DEVICE 3 2026-09-01 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
.SH NAME
llapi_find_device \- run lfs find's predicates over a scan of a target
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
.PP
.BI "int llapi_find_device(const char *" target ,
.BI "                      struct find_param *" param ,
.BI "                      const struct llapi_scan_param *" sp ");"
.PP
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B llapi_find_device()
applies the search in
.I param
to the objects of the MDT or OST named by
.IR target ,
which
.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
reads directly, and prints what matches.  The predicates are
.BR lfs-find (1)'s,
and it is what
.B lfs find --device
runs.
.SS What it prints
A match is printed by pathname, as a walk prints it, composed from the
target's names and parent FIDs (see NOTES), or counted as nameless and not
printed.
.I fp_print_fid
prints its FID instead, as %LF prints it; for an OST object that is the FID
of the file that owns it:
.IP
.EX
0x200000401:0x1:0x0
.EE
.LP
An object carrying no
.B trusted.lma
has recorded no FID of its own. On MDT0000 it still has one: its inode number
and generation are an IGIF, which is built and printed as the FID it is. That
is the only target where an IGIF can be built. On another MDT the object
may be FID-on-OST, or carry a FID that a file-level restore has invalidated,
and on an OST the object id is in the directory entry that names the inode,
not in the inode. Where no FID can be had, the
object's own id on the target is printed instead:
.IP
.EX
obj:1442
.EE
.LP
Only
.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_INTERNAL
reaches those, so an
.I fp_print_fid
search without it prints the first form and nothing else. Those objects have
no pathname, so without
.I fp_print_fid
they are counted as nameless and not printed.
Every record carries one or the other: the scan sets the object id on all
of them, so the second form always applies where the first does not.
.I fp_zero_end
in
.I param
chooses the separator (a NUL rather than a newline) as it does for
a walk.
.LP
.I sp
is the parameter block
.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
takes, or NULL for its defaults, so a knob added there needs no change here.
.I lfsp_flags
carries
.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_INTERNAL
as it does for a scan.  Three of its fields are the search's to decide and
are overwritten whatever the caller set:
.IR lfsp_want ,
.I lfsp_thread_count
and
.IR lfsp_filter .
.I lfsp_stats
is filled as
.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
fills it, so a program can see that part of the target was skipped.
Its
.I ss_emitted
counts the objects the search judged, not the ones it matched.
.I lfsp_got
is left untouched rather than filled for the caller: it would report the
demand mask the search settled on and not the one the caller asked for.
.I lfsp_filter
goes because there is no caller data to pair a filter with: the
scan carries this function's own state, and a caller's callback would be
handed that.
.LP
A target holds objects and not a namespace, so a search that needs one is
refused rather than answered wrongly: see ERRORS.  A size or a block count
that lives on the OSTs cannot be settled from an MDT alone, and objects a
predicate could not decide are counted and reported once at the end rather
than printed as matches.
.LP
A time predicate is not among those. For a striped regular file
.BR -atime ", " -mtime ", " -ctime
and
.B -newerXY
are answered from the MDT inode's copy, which the OSTs may have moved past:
over a mount the same predicate forces a glimpse, and a scan has none. The
object is answered rather than counted as undecided, so the result can
differ from
.BR llapi_find (3)
without saying that it did.
.LP
.B -name
differs the other way, by answering more rather than less. A walk meets a
hardlinked file once per link and tests the name it arrived by; a scan meets
the object once and tests the pattern against every name in its
.IR trusted.link ,
so a file matching under any of its names is reported once. An object with
no linkea has no name to test (an OST data object, and the
.BR CLS_INTERNAL ,
.B CLS_NO_LMA
and
.B CLS_AGENT
records
.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_INTERNAL
delivers) and is counted undecided rather than answered. The filesystem
root and
.IR .lustre ,
which have no linkea either, are neither matched nor counted.
An encrypted object is counted undecided too: its
.I trusted.link
holds the names the client encrypted. That includes the top directory of
an encrypted tree, whose own name is clear, since a record does not say
which object that is.
A file with too many links for its
.I trusted.link
to hold is counted undecided when none of its stored names matches, since
the MDT does not store the names past that limit.
.LP
A striped directory has a shard on each MDT it is striped over. The shards are
not reported: the master answers for the directory, once, as a walk does.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.B llapi_find_device()
returns:
.TP
0
on success.
.TP
<0
a negative errno on failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
.B -EINVAL
.I target
or
.I param
is NULL, or
.I lfsp_size
is outside the range this library accepts: shorter than its minimum,
longer than 4096 bytes, or longer than its own definition with a byte past the
end of it set. Also when
.I fp_print_fid
is set together with
.IR fp_format_printf_str ,
when
.I fp_fid2path_mnt
is set and nothing is printed by name
.RI ( fp_print_fid ,
or a format without
.BR %p ).
.TP 15
.B -ENOTSUP
this build has no scanning backend, or the search needs something a target
scan does not have. The two share this value, so a caller that skips its
work where a build cannot scan at all must not also skip it on a node that
can. See
.BR llapi_scan_device (3),
whose return value this function passes back unchanged, and which separates
the two. What a target scan does not have is
a mounted filesystem
.RB ( --xattr ),
or a walk to describe
.RB ( --maxdepth
other than 0,
.BR --mindepth ", " --threads ).
.B --ost
over an MDT and
.B --mdt
over an OST are refused with this value too: an OST object names its MDT only
through an FLD lookup, which needs a client, and which OSTs a file is striped
over is a question about its layout rather than about the target being read.
The layout options
.RB ( --stripe-count ", " --stripe-size ", " --extension-size ,
.BR --pool ", " --layout ", " --comp-* ", " --mirror-* ", " --foreign )
over an OST are refused too, because an OST object has no layout, and so are
.B --ost
and
.B --mdt
over a target whose label carries no index.
A search runs the scan single-threaded, since
.I fp_lmd
is one buffer for the whole of it, which is why
.I lfsp_thread_count
is among the fields above rather than a knob.
.TP 15
.B -ENAMETOOLONG
.I fp_fid2path_mnt
is longer than the buffer the mount path is held in.
.TP 15
.B -ENOTDIR
a format with
.B %p
asks for a pathname,
.I fp_fid2path_mnt
is not set, and
.I target
holds no directories to compose one from (an OST). Its objects are
named through a mount with
.IR fp_fid2path_mnt ,
or printed by FID with
.IR fp_print_fid .
Also returned when a component of
.I target
is not a directory.
.TP 15
.B -EXDEV
.I target
belongs to another filesystem than the one it is compared with: that of
.I fp_fid2path_mnt
where the search has one, and
.I lfsp_fsname
otherwise.
.TP 15
.B -ENOENT
.I target
does not exist.
.TP 15
.B -ENOPKG
the backend refused the target.
.TP 15
.B -EIO
the target could not be read.
.LP
Any other error
.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
returns is passed back as well;
.BR -EACCES ,
a device the caller cannot read, is the one a non-root caller meets first.
.SH NOTES
On an MDT, the search keeps every directory the scan meets (FID to parent and
name), and composes a match's pathname by walking those to the root, with no
lookup. A match met before a directory of its path waits for it, in memory up
to 256 MiB and then in a temporary file, and is printed when that directory
arrives, so the output is not in the scan's order. Only when a pathname is
printed: not for
.I fp_print_fid
or a format without
.BR %p .
After the scan, a directory it never met is looked up once through
.IR fp_fid2path_mnt ,
or without it, for root and a target mounted on this node, through a client
mount of the whole filesystem found there: under DNE, one on another MDT. A target being scanned is
never asked. An object that still cannot be placed has no pathname:
.B .lustre
and, with no mount to ask, under DNE, anything whose ancestors live on
another MDT. Such an object
is counted and reported rather than printed. A name in a directory whose
names are encrypted (its fscrypt context, read with
.BR LLAPI_SCAN_NAMES_ENC )
is printed as a client built with Lustre's own llcrypt lists it without the
key: base64url, or for a name longer than two blocks '+' and a digest of the
FID and the name. The pathnames are relative to
the filesystem root, which is printed as
.B .
(or as the mount with
.IR fp_fid2path_mnt ),
so a file is
.BR ./dir/file .
.LP
.I fp_fid2path_mnt
is a client mount
.B of the filesystem the target belongs to.
A subdirectory (fileset) mount is the root the paths start at, and a match
outside it is left out, as fid2path through that mount answers.
It is the one part of this call that needs a client. On an MDT it supplies
the prefix the composed paths hang from, the filesystem to compare the
target's label with, and the lookups above. On an OST there are no
directories, and an object is named by its owner's
.B trusted.link
read through the mount, with the owner's directories looked up once each and
kept. The filesystem of the mount is compared with the target's label, and a
target of any other filesystem is refused with
.BR -EXDEV .
A search without the mount and a plain
.BR llapi_scan_device ()
caller have no mount, and set
.I lfsp_fsname
on
.I sp
for the same check.
.LP
Without
.IR fp_fid2path_mnt ,
an MDT's pathnames come from the target alone, which is what names the objects
of a filesystem that is entirely down. An OST object is named by the file that
owns it, and that name lives on an MDT, so an OST prints FIDs as for
.IR fp_print_fid ,
and a format with
.B %p
is refused with
.BR -ENOTDIR .
.LP
.I param
is modified: the search runs with
.I fp_lazy
set, since a scan cannot glimpse an OST, and the buffers the search
allocates in it are released before returning.  The caller's
.I fp_lazy
is put back.
With
.BR --mdt ,
as in a walk,
.I fp_mdt_indexes
is left allocated and
.IR fp_mdt_index " and " fp_file_mdt_index
are changed.
.LP
.B --ost
and
.B --mdt
are about the target and not about each object: every object an MDT scan
delivers is on that MDT, and every object an OST scan delivers is on that OST.
The names given are resolved against the target's own label, so an index and
the target's UUID both work without a mount. A name for some other target is
not an error: the search simply matches nothing, and its negation matches
everything, which is what lets one predicate sweep a list of devices and skip
the ones that cannot hold a match. An empty result returns 0.
.LP
.B -printf
is answered from the record and not from a path, so every directive but
.B %p
works here.
.B %p
prints the same pathname as the default output.
.B %LF
prints the FID the record carries, and nothing for an object the scan has no
FID for.
For an OST data object it prints the FID of the file the object belongs to,
from its
.BR trusted.fid ,
and nothing for one no file owns yet.
.B %LO
prints an OST data object's own FID, in brackets, and the FIDs of a file's OST
objects from its layout;
the layout directives
.RB ( %Lc ", " %Li ", " %Lo ", " %Lp ", " %LS )
read the
.B trusted.lov
the scan already read, and print nothing for an object that has none or whose
layout did not parse. The filesystem default a predicate compares such an
object against is not a layout to print. An object with no project id prints
.B %LP
as 0, which is what a walk off Lustre prints for one as well.
.BR %s ", " %b " and " %k
print nothing for an object the scan has no size or blocks for, such as a
striped file with no
.BR trusted.som .
For a striped directory
.BR %n ,
.BR %a ,
.B %c
and
.B %t
print nothing too: the target holds the master inode's link count and
times, where a walk reports the directory's own, counted over every stripe.
.LP
.I fp_max_depth
may be 0, as
.BR llapi_find_param_alloc (3)
leaves it, or
.BR "(unsigned int)-1" .
A scan has no depth, so both mean no limit.
Any other value is refused.
.SH EXAMPLES
To print the regular files over a gigabyte on a stopped MDT, without
mounting it. A file whose size the MDT does not hold, such as a striped
file with no
.BR trusted.som ,
is counted as undecided and not printed:
.EX
        struct find_param *param = llapi_find_param_alloc();
        int rc;
\&
        if (param == NULL)
                return \-ENOMEM;
        param\->fp_max_depth  = (unsigned int)\-1;
        param\->fp_check_size = 1;
        param\->fp_size       = 1024ULL * 1024 * 1024;
        param\->fp_size_sign  = \-1;       /* greater than; '+size' is \-1 */
        param\->fp_type       = S_IFREG;
\&
        rc = llapi_find_device("/dev/vdb", param, NULL);
        llapi_find_param_free(param);
.EE
.LP
.I sp
is NULL here, so the scan runs with
.BR llapi_scan_device (3)'s
defaults; pass one with
.I lfsp_flags
set to
.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_INTERNAL
to search the target's own objects as well as the visible ones.
.SH AVAILABILITY
.B llapi_find_device()
is part of the
.BR lustre (7)
user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
.\" Added in commit v2_17_57-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
.SH SEE ALSO
